# fancyCat

A small Node.js command-line tool that extends `cat` by showing line numbers and a total line count.

## Section 1 — Command Description

**What it does:** `fancyCat` prints the contents of a file with each line numbered (right-aligned), then prints the total number of lines at the end.

**How to run it:**

```bash
node fancyCat.js <filename>
```

Example:

```bash
$ node fancyCat.js fruits.txt
1 | apple
2 | banana
3 | cherry

Total lines: 3
```

Requires [Node.js](https://nodejs.org/) (no other dependencies).

**What commands it combines:** It extends `cat` (display file contents) with the behavior of `cat -n` (line numbers) and `wc -l` (line count), in one command.

**Edge cases handled:**

| Situation | Behavior |
|---|---|
| No filename given | Prints usage message, exits with code 1 |
| File does not exist | Prints a "not found" error, exits with code 1 |
| Path is a directory | Prints an error, exits with code 1 |
| Empty file | Prints `(empty file)` and `Total lines: 0` |
| No trailing newline | Last line is still counted |
| Windows (CRLF) line endings | Handled correctly |

## Section 2 — AI-Assisted Programming
I asked AI to help me design a "fancy" command and to suggest test cases and
edge cases for it, using prompts like "What are some test cases I should use
for this command?" and "What edge cases could cause this program to fail?"
I also asked it for a draft implementation of fancyCat in Node.js and for
help with the Git commands needed to create the repository.

AI gave me a working starting point for the script and suggested the test
scenarios: a normal file, a longer file to check number alignment, an empty
file, a missing file, no argument, a directory, and a file with no trailing
newline. It also pointed out edge cases I probably would not have thought of
on my own, like the extra blank "line" that appears at the end of every file
when you split on newlines. It also walked me through the git init / add /
commit / push steps.


I had to run every test myself, since AI cannot run my code, and compare the
actual output to the expected output. I also decided how the output should
look (right-aligned line numbers with a "|" separator and a total at the
end) and what should happen in each edge case, such as printing "(empty file)"
instead of nothing.

The first version split lines only on "\n", so files with Windows-style
"\r\n" line endings could leave stray "\r" characters in the output. This
was fixed by splitting with /\r?\n/.
